# Converted to the revised ten-section template (plus section 11); content and tables carried from the previous version.
BOOK = 'Philemon'
TESTAMENT = 'New Testament'
GENRE = 'letters'
SUBTITLE = 'A Brother Beloved, Received as Paul Himself'
SECTIONS = [(1, 'Book Setting & Program Identity', [('p', 'This section introduces you to the book by explaining who wrote it, who received it, and what was happening when it was written. It also shows you which of God’s programs the book belongs to — Israel’s prophetic program or the Body of Christ’s mystery program. Knowing the setting and the program identity helps you read the book in its proper context and understand how its teachings fit into the larger story of Scripture.'), ('p', 'Philemon is a short letter from “Paul, a prisoner of Jesus Christ, and Timothy our brother” to “Philemon our dearly beloved, and fellowlabourer,” with Apphia, Archippus, and the church in his house (Philem. 1–2). Paul appeals for Onesimus, whom he calls his son begotten in his bonds, and asks Philemon to receive him as a brother beloved (Philem. 10, 16).'), ('box', 'Program Identity:', 'Philemon is a personal letter from Paul to a believer and his household church. It is read for what it says about love, reconciliation, and Christian fellowship. This reading rests on the letter’s own audience markers and setting.')]),
(2, 'Narrative Flow of Philemon', [('p', 'This section gives you a simple walkthrough of how the book unfolds. Instead of diving into details right away, it shows the major movements in the letter — how Paul begins, how his instructions develop, and how the letter builds toward its close. Seeing the flow of the book helps you understand the big picture before you study the individual passages.'), ('h3', 'Movement 1 — Greeting and Thanksgiving (vv. 1–7)'), ('p', 'Paul greets Philemon and the church in his house and gives thanks for his love and faith toward the Lord Jesus and toward all saints (Philem. 1–7).'), ('h3', 'Movement 2 — The Appeal for Onesimus (vv. 8–16)'), ('p', 'Paul beseeches Philemon for love’s sake on behalf of Onesimus, now profitable, sent back, and to be received no longer merely as a servant but as a brother beloved (Philem. 8–16).'), ('h3', 'Movement 3 — Receive Him as Myself (vv. 17–25)'), ('p', 'Paul asks Philemon to receive Onesimus as himself, offers to repay any loss, expresses confidence in him, and closes with grace (Philem. 17–25).')]),
(3, 'Program Themes in Philemon', [('p', 'This section highlights the major ideas Paul emphasizes in this book — the big doctrinal themes that shape how the Body of Christ should think and live. These themes are not verse-by-verse explanations; they are the book’s main emphases, the repeated ideas Paul wants believers to carry forward. Seeing these themes together helps you understand the book’s message before you look at the individual truths in the tables.'), ('ol', ['<b>Love and Faith Toward All</b> — Paul hears of Philemon’s love and faith toward the Lord Jesus and all saints (Philem. 5).', '<b>An Appeal Rather Than a Command</b> — Paul, though bold enough to enjoin, rather beseeches for love’s sake (Philem. 8–9).', '<b>A Brother Beloved</b> — Onesimus is to be received “not now as a servant, but above a servant, a brother beloved” (Philem. 16).', '<b>Partnership</b> — “If thou count me therefore a partner, receive him as myself” (Philem. 17).', '<b>Refreshed Hearts</b> — Paul wants Philemon to refresh his bowels in the Lord (Philem. 20).'])]),
(4, 'Christ in Philemon', [('p', 'This section gathers what the letter teaches about Christ — who He is, what He has done, and how Paul presents Him to the church. These statements help you see the book’s Christ-centered focus before you study the doctrinal truths in detail. By collecting these verses together, you get a clear picture of how Philemon lifts up Christ.'), ('ol', ['<b>Our Lord Jesus Christ</b> — Grace and peace come from God our Father and the Lord Jesus Christ (Philem. 3).', '<b>The Lord Jesus</b> — Philemon’s love and faith are toward the Lord Jesus (Philem. 5).', '<b>In Christ</b> — Paul speaks of “much bold in Christ” and of the good that is in them “in Christ Jesus” (Philem. 6, 8).', '<b>In the Lord</b> — Paul appeals to Philemon to refresh his heart “in the Lord” (Philem. 20).', '<b>The Grace of Our Lord</b> — The letter closes: “The grace of our Lord Jesus Christ be with your spirit” (Philem. 25).'])]),
(5, 'The Church, the Law, and Israel in Philemon', [('p', 'This section helps you see how Philemon speaks about the church, the law, and Israel without mixing their programs. By gathering these points together, this section shows you how to read Philemon in its own setting — as a Body-of-Christ letter — while recognizing what it says (and does not say) about Israel’s program.'), ('p', 'The letter is a personal appeal and speaks of no covenant or program:'), ('ul', ['Paul calls himself “a prisoner of Jesus Christ” (Philem. 1, 9).', 'The letter names Philemon, Apphia, Archippus, and the church in his house (Philem. 1–2).', 'The letter does not mention Israel and does not place believers inside any covenant.', 'The letter does not make a doctrine of slavery; it reports Paul’s appeal for one man, Onesimus.']), ('p', 'This reading rests on the letter’s own audience markers and setting.')]),
(6, 'Body-of-Christ Distinctions', [('p', 'This section highlights the teachings in Philemon that belong uniquely to the Body of Christ — truths Paul gives to the church in this present age. By gathering these together, this section helps you see how Philemon contributes to the doctrine and order of the Body of Christ without borrowing from Israel’s prophetic program.'), ('p', 'Philemon shows the Body’s fellowship in Christ in practice:'), ('ul', ['a church gathered in a house', 'love and faith toward the Lord Jesus and all saints', 'a brother received above a servant', 'partnership and substitution offered in love', 'grace closing the letter']), ('p', 'It does not give a general rule on household relations beyond what Paul writes here.')]),
(7, 'Truths Table: What Philemon Says', [('p', 'This table gathers the key statements, instructions, and teachings in Philemon and shows which ones belong to the Body of Christ. Each row lists a truth from the letter, explains what it says, and identifies its program identity based on Paul’s audience and setting. This helps you see, at a glance, what Philemon teaches directly to the church today and keeps those truths separate from universal principles or reports that apply more broadly.'), ('p', 'Program Identity shows where a truth belongs. “Body of Christ (Pauline) Truth” marks a statement, command, warning or instruction given to the Body of Christ through Paul.'), ('table', ['Category', 'Truth Revealed in Philemon', 'Program Identity', 'Scripture'], [['Love and Faith', 'Love and faith toward the Lord Jesus and all saints.', 'Body of Christ (Pauline) Truth', 'Philem. 5'], ['Fellowship of Faith', 'The communication of thy faith becomes effectual.', 'Body of Christ (Pauline) Truth', 'Philem. 6'], ['Appeal for Love’s Sake', 'Paul beseeches rather than commands.', 'Body of Christ (Pauline) Truth', 'Philem. 8–9'], ['A Brother Beloved', 'Onesimus is more than a servant, a brother beloved.', 'Body of Christ (Pauline) Truth', 'Philem. 16'], ['Partnership', 'Receive him as myself.', 'Body of Christ (Pauline) Truth', 'Philem. 17'], ['Substitution Offered', 'Put that on mine account; I will repay it.', 'Body of Christ (Pauline) Truth', 'Philem. 18–19'], ['A House Church', 'The church in thy house.', 'Body of Christ (Pauline) Truth', 'Philem. 2'], ['Appeal as a Prisoner', 'Paul appeals as the aged and now a prisoner of Jesus Christ.', 'Body of Christ (Pauline) Truth', 'Philem. 9']], [20, 36, 26, 18])]),
(8, 'Universal Truths Table (All People, All Time)', [('p', 'This table gathers the truths in Philemon that apply to everyone, not just the Body of Christ. These are principles rooted in God’s character — things that hold true across all programs and all ages. By separating these universal truths from Paul’s program-specific instructions, this section helps you see what Philemon teaches all believers everywhere and what it teaches the church in its own setting.'), ('table', ['Universal Truth', 'Description', 'Scripture'], [['Thanksgiving in Prayer', 'I thank my God, making mention of thee always in my prayers.', 'Philem. 4'], ['Refresh the Saints', 'The bowels of the saints are refreshed by thee, brother.', 'Philem. 7'], ['Love’s Appeal', 'For love’s sake I rather beseech thee.', 'Philem. 9'], ['Willing Service', 'Do not let thy benefit be as it were of necessity, but willingly.', 'Philem. 14'], ['Receive as a Brother', 'Receive him forever, a brother beloved.', 'Philem. 15–16'], ['Bear a Debt', 'If he hath wronged thee, or oweth thee ought, put that on mine account.', 'Philem. 18'], ['Obedience', 'Having confidence in thy obedience I wrote unto thee.', 'Philem. 21'], ['Prayer and Hope', 'I trust that through your prayers I shall be given unto you.', 'Philem. 22']], [30, 46, 24])]),
(9, 'Program-Safe Application Grid', [('p', 'This section shows how to apply the teachings of Philemon without mixing programs. The applications come directly from Paul’s instructions to the Body of Christ and from the universal truths that apply to everyone. By keeping the applications tied to what the letter actually says — and not borrowing commands from Israel’s covenants or prophetic program — this grid helps you respond to Philemon in a way that is faithful to its setting, clear, and safe.'), ('ol', ['<b>Love all the saints</b> — Show love and faith toward all (Philem. 5).', '<b>Share faith in practice</b> — Let fellowship become effectual (Philem. 6).', '<b>Appeal in love</b> — Persuade rather than command (Philem. 9).', '<b>Receive as a brother</b> — Welcome one who has been apart (Philem. 15–16).', '<b>Bear another’s debt</b> — Stand beside one who owes (Philem. 18–19).', '<b>Refresh one another</b> — Give joy in the Lord (Philem. 7, 20).', '<b>Give willingly</b> — Do good freely, not by compulsion (Philem. 14).', '<b>Pray for one another</b> — Hold hope together in prayer (Philem. 22).'])]),
(10, 'Teaching Outline for Philemon', [('p', 'This outline gives you a teacher-ready structure for presenting the book. It follows the narrative movements and highlights the major themes, making it easy to teach Philemon in classes, small groups, or personal study.'), ('olr', ['Thanksgiving (vv. 1–7) — Love and faith toward all. <i>(Philem. 4–7)</i>', 'Love’s Appeal (vv. 8–11) — Paul beseeches for Onesimus. <i>(Philem. 8–11)</i>', 'Sent Back (vv. 12–14) — Not by necessity, but willingly. <i>(Philem. 12–14)</i>', 'A Brother Beloved (vv. 15–16) — Received above a servant. <i>(Philem. 15–16)</i>', 'As Myself (vv. 17–19) — Partnership and substitution. <i>(Philem. 17–19)</i>', 'Refresh My Heart (vv. 20–22) — Confidence and a hope of visiting. <i>(Philem. 20–22)</i>', 'Greetings and Grace (vv. 23–25) — Fellowlabourers and the grace of our Lord. <i>(Philem. 23–25)</i>'])]),
(11, 'Companion Usage and Study Suggestions', [('h3', 'How to Use This Companion in Personal Study'), ('p', 'This Companion is designed to walk alongside you as you read Philemon, helping you see both what the letter says and the timeless truths rooted in God’s character. Use it slowly, chapter by chapter, letting the narrative movements, themes, and truths deepen your understanding. The Program-Safe Applications draw only on what Philemon itself says, so your response stays tied to the text.'), ('h3', 'How to Use This Companion in Small Groups or Classes'), ('p', 'This Companion provides a clear, structured framework that makes teaching Philemon simple and accessible. Each section gives you ready-to-use material: narrative movements for overview, program themes for doctrinal clarity, universal truths for discussion, and a pastoral teaching outline for weekly lessons. Use the tables to help your group see what the letter states and what it leaves unsaid, and use the Program-Safe Application Grid to foster meaningful, Christ-centered conversation.'), ('h3', 'How This Companion Fits Within the Acts Overlap Theology Series'), ('p', 'This Companion is part of a larger series designed to help believers read every book of Scripture without blurring God’s prophetic program for Israel and His mystery program for the Body of Christ. Within the Acts Overlap framework, Philemon was written during the overlap as a personal letter from Paul. This Companion shows you how to read the appeal in its own setting while drawing out the principles that apply to all believers.')])]

